Animate4U: A Blender Animation Add-on

Hi, I'm Gregory Turner, a Master of Science in Computer Science student at Full Sail University and the creator of Animate4U. 
My background sits at the intersection of software engineering, human-computer interaction, and 2D/3D character art, building 
character rigs, turnaround sheets, expression sets, and stylized visuals in Blender and adjacent creative tools. 
Animators spend enormous energy polishing curves, protecting key poses, and recovering from awkward interpolation artifacts 
when they should be focusing on acting, timing, and storytelling.

My goal with Animate4U is to build a fast, non-destructive Blender 5.0 add-on that assists animators directly inside the 3D 
Viewport without getting in their way or locking them into fragile black boxes. Over the past month, I made a pivotal scope 
decision: streamlining the project by removing the heavy ML pipeline and Normal-priority extras so I could ship a rock-solid, 
deterministic math-and-curve workflow for Milestone 1 and 2. Whenever self-doubt creeps in, especially when debugging Blender's 
Python runtime or preparing human-subjects evaluation materials, coming back to why I started this build helps ground me. 
Reliable tools make creative iteration faster and safer.

This month focused on building and wiring together the core Milestone 1 and 2 architecture inside Visual Studio Code for 
Blender 5.0:

3D Viewport Sidebar Panel: Organized the add-on UI around three primary workflow pillars—Smoothing, Smart Breakdowns, and 
Secondary Motion, paired with a live 0.0–1.0 Assistance Strength slider and a one-click Before/After toggle for instant 
visual comparison.

Fast Curve I/O & 360-Flip Prevention (core/fcurve_io.py): Built fast NumPy-backed curve reading and writing routines that 
guard against rotational 360-degree flips during curve processing so angular channels stay continuous and predictable.

In-Memory Backup Cache & Extreme Keyframe Locking (core/state_cache.py): Implemented an in-memory curve backup cache so 
animators can revert safely at any time, alongside manual EXTREME keyframe locking to protect critical pose extremes from 
being flattened or drifted during edits.

Pin-Locked Curve Smoothing & Handle Fixer (core/smoothing.py): Developed a SciPy-based smoothing solver engineered to stay 
within 0.001 units of pin-locked constraints and keep curve evaluation responsive in under 15 ms on the live slider path, 
supported by curve handle adjustments.

I built these modules using Python, NumPy, SciPy, and Blender's Python API, referencing Blender's F-Curve/action data 
structures, SciPy interpolation/optimization documentation, and my updated High-Level Design Document. 
The hardest technical challenges this month were wiring VS Code's Python environment to Blender 5.0's embedded Python runtime, 
testing add-on execution cleanly, keeping Git commit history tidy, and tuning solver responsiveness so live scrubbing feels 
immediate.

What Went Right This Month:
Shipping Core M1 Modules: Getting the sidebar layout, core/fcurve_io.py, core/state_cache.py, core/smoothing.py, 
EXTREME keyframe locking, Before/After toggle, and handle/slider workflow into place gave me a tangible, 
testable system rather than just diagrams.

Cutting Scope Decisively: Dropping the ML pipeline and Normal-priority backlog kept development focused on high-value curve 
math and usability readiness.

Evaluation Prep: Completing IRB training and drafting study protocols, informed consent documents, schedules, and 
presentation materials moved the capstone from pure coding toward real user validation.

What Went Wrong This Month:
Environment & Tooling Friction: Aligning VS Code editor/linting/execution paths with Blender 5.0's Python environment and 
smoothing out Git commit workflows took extra debugging time.

Over-Ambitious Initial Scope: Carrying ML and secondary features too far into planning initially created split focus before 
the core curve math was proven.

How I Can Improve Moving Forward
Lock down repeatable Blender add-on test scripts and clean Git commit habits early in each sprint so environment setup 
never slows feature work.

Time-box documentation and slide polish separately from coding blocks so neither gets squeezed at the last minute.
